Description: mi: make the address-validation pipe thread-local
 _mem_validate_pipe was a process-global pipe whose lazy initialisation was
 guarded by an atomic_flag. A thread losing the test-and-set race could read()
 from fd -1, get EBADF and call _open_pipe(), closing the shared descriptors
 while other threads were writing to them. unw_step() then fails spuriously;
 in .NET (which links the system libunwind on Ubuntu amd64) this aborts the
 process with "Fatal error. Internal CLR error. (0x80131506)".
 .
 Give each thread its own pipe and drop the global initialisation flag.
 This is the Gaddress_validator.c part of upstream PR #993; the rest of that PR
 (_Thread_local -> thread_local rename and threads.h detection) is not needed.
Author: libunwind upstream
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libunwind/libunwind/pull/993
Bug: https://github.com/libunwind/libunwind/issues/715
Bug-Ubuntu: https://bugs.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+bug/2163034
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-10-13
---
--- a/src/mi/Gaddress_validator.c
+++ b/src/mi/Gaddress_validator.c
@@ -35,11 +35,9 @@ unw_address_is_valid(UNUSED unw_word_t a
 
 #else /* !UNW_REMOTE_ONLY */
 
-#include <stdatomic.h>
-
-
-static atomic_flag _unw_address_validator_initialized = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT;
-static int _mem_validate_pipe[2] = {-1, -1};
+/* Each thread manages its own pipe so concurrent callers (including signal
+ * handlers) never race on closing or reopening another thread's fds. */
+static _Thread_local int _mem_validate_pipe[2] = {-1, -1};
 
 #ifdef HAVE_PIPE2
 static int
@@ -104,7 +102,7 @@ _write_validate (unw_word_t addr)
   int ret = -1;
   ssize_t bytes = 0;
 
-  if (unlikely (!atomic_flag_test_and_set(&_unw_address_validator_initialized)))
+  if (unlikely (_mem_validate_pipe[0] == -1))
     {
       if (_open_pipe () != 0)
         {
